 moment i  also think that as i as i mentioned  before the peculiarity of beirut is that  it had a very weak state this is for  historical reasons the french which was  the imperial power and lebanon what was  not yet called Lebanon during the  Mandate period between the wars  basically it was the it was the the  poorer of the two imperial powers  Britain and France in the region and it  ran the mandate on a kind of shoestring  budget  so it starved the state of a lot of  resources also Lebanon is a country with  lots of  different confessional groups and Druze  Maronite Christians Shias and Sunnis  Armenians and the leaders of those  communities were often happy not to have  the state take over things like  education and health services so they  were happy to also star of the state and  in some ways or keep it from becoming  very powerful so one of the knock-on  effects of this history is that as I  said censorship laws were relatively  below and so there was an emerging and  very lively civil society in the sense  that there were journals there were  newspapers and they fought with each  other they didn't tow the state's party  line even if they were towing one or the  other confessional groups party line  let's say so there was a kind of  effervescence to intellectual life  during this period that had to do with  the peculiar history of Lebanon itself  and poetry though was hugely popular  among all different classes of people  there in Lebanon yes which is somewhat  unusual compared to other countries I  would say even more broadly than that  that you know poetry is the oldest Arab  art form we have really I mean we have  poems from the pre-islamic period so  it's a it's a form with a with a  fourteen fifteen hundred year history  behind it and so it's a kind of the  queen of the arts and it has been so  that when the Arab Spring occurred in  2011 it was not terribly surprising that  the great slogan of that moment shot ud  despot the Nizam the people want the  downfall of the regime is actually taken  borrowed from adapted from a poem by a  Tunisian poet named Al Qasimi should be  because poetry has this tremendous aura  and status as both an art form with a  long history as well as contemporary  resonance as well as popular following  those  the other audience and that's something  that I think it takes a little while for  Americans to wrap their heads around  that this is that this is a form that  while it was I think entering into a  kind of moment of crisis after World War  two and and that crisis has continued it  is still a form which had great which  had great status and great great elite  literary status as well as a popular  audience and in some ways we can only  think about somebody like you know  Shakespeare enjoying in our own culture  when you're reading the poems I imagine  of course they're in Arabic yes what was  it like for you to and I obviously know  you read Arabic how was it like to think  about the poetry while written in Arabic  but translating it into English was that  very difficult yeah that's a great  question I mean it it's um I do a lot of  translating in the book because I do  close readings of poems and to do those  close readings when it has to have the  text there next to you and of course  you're writing your commentary in  English but you're writing about a poem  that has been written in Arabic so you  have to kind of signpost the translation  with with the Arabic words that you  might be talking about and it's very  tricky and you have to be very careful  about a choosey about which poems you're  going to look closely at because you  also I also want you know part of the  the stakes of the book I would say is to  persuade possibly skeptical American or  English language readers that this is a  great poetical tradition and it's  something that we can learn from and  it's interesting for us to read so I did  want to choose poems that I thought are  great poems that are interesting and  then also have something to say you know  that I can say clearly and cleanly about  the way the language works so I think  you have to be I think you just have to  be especially careful about what texts  you choose because you have to  be able to explain the enormous  intricacy of what's going on in the  Arabic in English right  and a lot of times there are some words  that there are simply no English words  for correct  you mean like untranslatable yes  I'm not a great fan of the idea of  untranslated bility I have to say I mean  I think that all words are in some ways  translatable there are certain words  that maybe need paraphrasing rather than  strict one-to-one sorts of  correspondence I mean that is true but I  don't think that translation is really  captured by the idea of substituting a  word in one language for a word in  another language I think really the  units of sense and aesthetic sense that  we're talking about when we talk about  doing translation are much broader than  merely a word which which we might very  well have no equivalent for in English  so there are words that I talk about in  the book like the word caught up is the  most famous one of these thought up  which means something like musical  ecstasy the feeling of musical ecstasy  which is shared between a performer and  an audience and it becomes a very  important word for discussing notions of  meter and music in modern modernist  poetry versus more traditional poetry so  how to translate table is is a question  I look at very briefly but I think in  the end if commentary is also doing the  work of translation I think you know  that's just your job right right okay  good so what would you like your reader  to take away from your book well I would  like them to as I was saying you know  I'd like them to take away the idea that  this is a an immensely interesting rich  corpus of poetry that probably most  American readers are unfamiliar with I  also hope that you know those who are  interested in in poetry generally  speaking by looking at this particular  movement might learn to look at the the  texts and verse that they are more  familiar with with new eyes mm-hmm okay  wonderful  thank you  so much for being here with us today and  sharing your work it was a pleasure for  more information about Professor  Cresswell and his research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and area studies at you  you
 

